// expect-error: NITPICK-RESOLVE-002
// expect-error-at: 26:19
// expect-note: NITPICK-MACRO-009
// expect-note-at: 30:15
//
// AND THE NOTE IS PART OF THE ASSERTION. A node in an expanded body was WRITTEN in
// the macro and INSTANTIATED at the call site, and its span carries only the first
// — so the error points at line 26, inside a macro nobody may have written, and the
// line that made it wrong is the invocation on line 30.
//
// `MACRO_REFERENCE.md` section 10 recorded this as open after 0.6.1 and 0.6.6 closes
// it: the finding keeps the body's location, because that is where the offending
// construct is, and a note carries the invocation. Both are true and the reader
// needs both.
//
// A NAME THAT EXISTS ONLY AT THE CALL SITE. The macro body reads `only_local`,
// which is a local of `main` and nothing in the scope the macro was written in.
//
// The prototype accepted this: it noticed the disagreement, emitted NITPICK-061 as
// a warning, and bound to the caller's `only_local` anyway. D-057 calls that what
// it is — "a warning is not a mechanism; it is a request that someone be paying
// attention" — and the flipped rule makes it an error with `#caller(only_local)`
// as the one way to say you meant it.
mod:hygiene;

macro:grab = () { only_local + 1i32; };

func:main = int32(cstring[]:_~argv) {
    int32:only_local = 3i32;
    int32:s = #grab();
    exit 0i32;
};
